<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lindanise</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1219">1219</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Valdemar · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lindanise was fought on 15 June 1219 at the site of present-day Tallinn, when the crusading army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II of Denmark</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> of Rävala. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> then built a castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toompea">Toompea</a>, around which the town of Reval grew. Legend holds that the Danish flag, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flag-of-denmark">Dannebrog</a>, fell from the sky during the battle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lindanise.jpg" alt="Battle of Lindanise" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian August Lorentzen · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danmarks_flag_1219_Lorentzen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-crusades">Northern Crusades</a>, fought between the invading Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and the non-Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II of Denmark</a> had undertaken his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in response to calls from the Pope. At the beginning of June 1219 he sailed to the northern Estonian province of Revalia with Archbishop Anders Sunesen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a>, Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theoderich-von-treyden">Theoderich von Treyden</a> and his vassals Count Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordalbingia">Nordalbingia</a> and Vitslav I of Rügen. The army camped at Lindanise and built a castle named Castrum Danorum. The Estonians sent negotiators, but only to gain time while they assembled an army large enough to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 15 June 1219 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> near the castle, right after supper, advancing from five directions. The crusaders were completely surprised and fled in all directions, and the Estonians killed Bishop Theoderich, believing he was the king. The Danes were saved by their Wendish vassals: Witslaw led a quick counterattack that stopped the Estonian advance, giving the crusaders time to regroup, and the Estonians were routed. The victory helped Valdemar II to claim northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> afterwards, at the site of the later Hanseatic city of Reval, now Tallinn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The battle is still well known among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> because of the legend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flag-of-denmark">Dannebrog</a>, the Danish flag. According to the legend, as the Danes were hard pressed, Archbishop Sunesen raised his hands in prayer and the defenders held while they stayed raised; when he tired and lowered them, the Estonians neared victory, and a red flag with a white cross fell from the sky and gave the Danes the win.</p><p>The account builds on two versions of the early 16th century, both from an older source. An edition of the Gesta Danorum published in 1514 placed the flag's appearance in a battle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> in 1208, and the Franciscan friar Peder Olsen, about 1527, corrected the year to 1219. The red flag with a white cross did originate as a crusader symbol.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Lyndanisse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Valdemar II “the Victorious” led a crusade to Estonia, where, according to a legend first recorded in the sixteenth century, the Dannebrog flag fell from heaven during the <b>Battle of Lyndanisse</b> in 1219.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1219 King Valdemar II of Denmark landed with a crusading army, defeated the Estonians at the <b>Battle of Lindanise</b>, the battle at which, according to later legend, the Danish flag, the Dannebrog, fell from heaven.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1219 Valdemar II of Denmark landed with a crusading army, defeated the Estonians at the <b>Battle of Lindanise</b> and built a castle on Toompea; legend places the falling of the Danish flag, the Dannebrog, from the sky at this battle.</blockquote><a href="/state/ravala">Rävala</a></li></ul></section>
